{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB03793 - MONDO_8231",
  "verbalization": "Benzoic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Peyronie Disease through a shared pathway involving Collagenase Clostridium Histolyticum. The evidence suggests that Benzoic Acid may act as a precursor or modulator in the metabolic processes that lead to the formation of collagenous tissue in Peyronie Disease. Both Balanitis Xerotica Obliterans and Penile Fibromatosis, which are conditions associated with Benzoic Acid, are linked to Collagenase Clostridium Histolyticum, which is known to play a role in the degradation of collagen and the development of Peyronie Disease. This suggests a potential mechanism where Benzoic Acid may influence the activity of Collagenase Clostridium Histolyticum, thereby contributing to the progression of Peyronie Disease. The presence of a common intermediary, Collagenase Clostridium Histolyticum, across multiple pathways supports the idea of a shared biological mechanism, although the exact nature of the interaction between Benzoic Acid and Collagenase remains to be fully elucidated.",
